<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Prüm</h1><p class="st-meta">721 CE – 1576 CE (approx.) · capital Prüm</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A great Carolingian abbey in the Eifel, the burial place of Emperor Lothair I and a rich imperial territory until Trier absorbed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The abbey was founded in 721 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrada-of-prum">Bertrada the Elder</a>, and refounded in 752 by her grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pippin the Short</a> and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrada-of-laon">Bertrada of Laon</a>, who gave it rich estates. Under Carolingian favour Prüm became one of the most important monasteries between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prum-urbar">Prüm Urbar</a> of 893, a register of estates and dues, is a key source on the Carolingian rural economy. Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regino-of-prum">Regino of Prüm</a> wrote an important chronicle there, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a> retired to the abbey and died there in 855. Later its abbot was a prince of the Empire ruling a small territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Prüm's possessions shrank from the 13th century, and the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> sought control over it. In 1576 the abbey was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-trier">Electorate of Trier</a>, the archbishop acting as its administrator. The monastery itself survived until French secularisation in 1802.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-prum.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Prüm" width="320" height="310"><figcaption>Image: Heiliges_Römisches_Reich_1400.png: Gustav Droysen derivative work: OwenBlacker · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichksabtei_Pr%C3%BCm.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>721 CE – 1576 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Prüm</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Regino of Prüm, Ansbald</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Middle High German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prüm Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1148&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-prum" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Prüm on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4827 432 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Prüm lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5147 688L5229 694M5147 688L5153 701"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5153 701h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5147 688h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5147 688h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1148&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-prum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
